{"name":"Diminished Lime","hex":"#edf5dd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"diminished","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of diminish"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["lessened, reduced","made to seem less important, impressive, or valuable"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/diminished"},{"term":"lime","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any inorganic material containing calcium, usually calcium oxide (quicklime) or calcium hydroxide (slaked lime).","Any gluey or adhesive substance that traps or captures; sometimes a synonym for birdlime."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To treat with calcium hydroxide or calcium oxide (lime).","To smear with birdlime.\n To ensnare, catch, entrap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lime"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lime (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lime is a color that is a shade of yellow-green, so named because it is a representation of the color of the citrus fruit lime. It is the color that is in between the web color chartreuse and yellow on the color wheel. Alternate names for this color included yellow-green, lemon-lime, lime green, or bitter lime."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg/330px-AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lime_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/The_Magdalen_Reading_-_Rogier_van_der_Weyden_%28cropped%2972.jpg/500px-The_Magdalen_Reading_-_Rogier_van_der_Weyden_%28cropped%2972.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/The_Magdalen_Reading_-_Rogier_van_der_Weyden_%28cropped%2972.jpg/500px-The_Magdalen_Reading_-_Rogier_van_der_Weyden_%28cropped%2972.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_camp